{"name":"Aragonite Blue","hex":"#6a95b1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aragonite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evaporite consisting of anhydrous calcium carbonate with the chemical formula CaCO3 and occurring in pearls, shells and nacre; it is dimorphous with calcite."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aragonite"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aragonite","blocks":[{"text":"Aragonite is a carbonate mineral and one of the three most common naturally occurring crystal forms of calcium carbonate, the others being calcite and vaterite. It is formed by biological and physical processes, including precipitation from marine and freshwater environments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Aragonite_crystal_-_Los_Molinillos%2C_Ceunca%2C_Spain_-_4x3.6x3.5cm_100g.jpg/330px-Aragonite_crystal_-_Los_Molinillos%2C_Ceunca%2C_Spain_-_4x3.6x3.5cm_100g.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aragonite","wikidata":"Q23775"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/AragoniteFromCrystalMaker.png/500px-AragoniteFromCrystalMaker.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/AragoniteFromCrystalMaker.png/500px-AragoniteFromCrystalMaker.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aragonite crystal structure","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAragoniteFromCrystalMaker.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Aragonito_Torrubia.jpg/500px-Aragonito_Torrubia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Aragonito_Torrubia.jpg/500px-Aragonito_Torrubia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hexagonal crystals (currently aragonite) from Molina de Aragón (Guadalajara), according to Torrubia (1754)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAragonito_Torrubia.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Aragonite_2_Enguidanos.jpg/330px-Aragonite_2_Enguidanos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Aragonite_2_Enguidanos.jpg/960px-Aragonite_2_Enguidanos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3